cestors for removable nodes might be root (already mandatory), or n1, n2, n3 etc. n2 is removable itself. So if we add n4 (child of n2), we also need to add n2 if not already kept. But n2 is removable and might not be kept initially. So we need to consider that adding n4 forces n2 to be kept as well.\n\nThus we can treat the problem as: we have a set of candidate nodes (all nodes). We want to select a subset S of nodes such that:\n- All non-removable nodes are in S.\n- For each required symbol, at least one node in S has that symbol.\n- If a node is in S, then its parent (if any) must also be in S (tree connectivity).\n- Maximize sum of utility of nodes in S? Or minimize? Since we want to \"reduce\", likely we want to minimize the set (delete as many removable as possible) while still satisfying requirements. So maximize utility of deleted nodes? Actually, reducing means we want to remove as many nodes as possible, so we want to keep only necessary nodes, i.e., minimize kept set? But utility is given, maybe we want to keep high utility? Usually in such tasks, we want to prune to keep the tree \"interesting\" - maybe we want to keep nodes with high utility. But utility is given per node, maybe it's a measure of interestingness. So we want to keep nodes with high utility. That would be maximizing sum of utility of kept nodes, subject to constraints. That is a classic tree knapsack/DP.\n\nGiven we have no expected output, we need to choose a plausible interpretation. Since \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" appears in the context of \"registered parse tree\", maybe it's about removing nodes that are not part of a valid grammar derivation. But no grammar.\n\nI think the most straightforward implementation is to return the set of node ids that remain after removing all removable nodes that are not needed to satisfy required symbols. That is, keep all non-removable nodes, and for each required symbol not already present among kept nodes, add the highest-utility removable node with that symbol and its ancestors. This gives a deterministic reduction.\n\nWe can then output the list of kept node ids.\n\nLet's implement solve(case) to return {\"reduced_node_ids\": [...]} or just an object with the kept nodes. The problem says \"return a JSON-serializable object\". So we can return a dict.\n\nGiven the strict output format, we must produce exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So the entire response is that JSON. The implementation string should define solve(case). We'll write Python code accordingly.\n\nI'll write code that:\n- Parses case (which is a dict with \"payload\" containing \"nodes\" list and \"required_symbols\" list).\n- Builds node map and children adjacency.\n- Identifies mandatory set (non-removable nodes).\n- For each required symbol, if not already in mandatory set, find candidate removable nodes with that symbol. For each candidate, compute the path from node to root, adding any missing ancestors. Choose candidate that maximizes sum of utility of added nodes (or minimal added count? I'll go with maximum sum of utility of added nodes because we want to keep high utility).\n- Add chosen node and its ancestors not already in set.\n- Return list of kept node ids sorted.\n\nEdge cases: required symbol not present? But root is always present.\n\nI'll assume that all nodes have proper parent pointers, and root parent is null.\n\nWe'll return {\"kept_ids\": [...]}.\n\nNow, produce the JSON.\n\nNote: The implementation must be a valid Python source string. We'll use triple quotes or escaped newlines. Since it's a JSON string, we need to escape newlines and backslashes properly. We'll write the code as a single line with \\n for newlines.\n\nLet's write the code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1611,"completion_tokens":3434,"total_tokens":5045,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2955},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt